# Actions & Handlers

`app/Services/` is NOT the domain layer (only release/export utilities). Domain work lives in Actions and Handlers.

## Actions (`app/Actions/*Actions.php`)

- Extend `App\Actions\Action`.
- Validate with `Validator::make(...)->validate()` (or Form Requests in HTTP controllers).
- Use `self::omitNullValues($data)` before create/update so null optionals do not wipe fields.
- Prefer instance methods when called via `Model::actions()`; static helpers OK for simple creates.
- Throw `ValidationException` for business-rule failures the UI should show.

```php
Cart::actions()->checkoutAsClient([...]);
Order::actions()->someMethod([...]);
```

Wire new Actions on the model with an `actions()` method returning `new FooActions` (follow existing models).

## Handlers (`app/Handlers/`)

- Invoked from `Payment::completed()` (and similar) via a stored `handler` class string + method like `onPaymentCompleted(Payment $payment)`.
- Known patterns: `CartCompletedHandler`, `Handlers\Cart\PackagePurchasedHandler`, `OrderRenewalHandler`, `BalanceTopupHandler`, `Handlers\Subscriptions\*`.
- Handlers orchestrate model methods / nested handlers — keep gateway/server API calls in Extensions.

## Events vs handlers

- Models may `$dispatchesEvents` for lifecycle; domain completion usually runs through **handlers**, not Event listeners (`EventServiceProvider` is largely empty).
- Queued provisioning: `app/Jobs/Orders/Order*Server` — dispatch from Order methods, do not block HTTP on panel APIs.

## Controllers

- Slim: authorize/permission middleware + call Actions or return theme views.
- Inject a Service into a method only if used once; constructor if used in multiple methods (project PHP rule). Prefer Actions over inventing new Services for billing/catalog.
